### DEVELOPMENT ###

# Frontend - in command prompt, navigate into the /frontend folder to run these commands.
# 1. Install the required frontend packages: npm install
# 2. Run the frontend: npm start
# Click the link to open the frontend on your web browser.

# Backend - in command prompt, navigate into the /backend folder to run these commands.
# 1. Install the required backend packages: npm install
# 2. Set up prisma: make migrate
# 3. (Optional) Seed the database with pre-populated data: make seed
# 4. Run the backend server: npm run start

### DEPLOYMENT ###
# These steps describe how to deploy the application Railway, a PaaS.
# 1. Create a GitHub repository containing this code.
# 2. Set up a Railway account by signing in with GitHub. Ensure that Railway is granted the necessary permissions to access your repostories.
# 3. On the Railway dashboard, create a new project and link it to the repository.

# 4. Create the frontend service by linking the GitHub repo, and changing the following settings:
#   Root directory: frontend/
#   Build command: npm run build
#   Start command: npx serve -s dist
# 5. Deploy the frontend.
# 6. After deployment, go to the settings tab. Under "Networking", generate a domain and set the port to be 8080.

# 7. Create the backend service by adding the GitHub repo, and changing the following settings:
#   Root directory: backend/
#   Start command: npm run start
# 8. In the variables tab, create a variable called FRONTEND_URL with the URL of the frontend domain that you generated earlier.
# 9. Deploy the backend.
# 10. After deployment, go to the settings tab. Under "Networking", generate a domain and set the port to be 8080.

# 11. Go back to the frontend service and add an environment variable VITE_BACKEND_URL with the URL of the backend domain you generated earlier.

# Now, the web application should be fully functional, and can be accessed via the public domain of the frontend service.